  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 412 PM EDT MON OCT 19 2026\n\nThere is reasonably high confidence in dry weather with normal to\nslightly above normal temperatures through the period.\n\nIn terms of our local area, there is good model agreement for the \nbig picture until near the end of the forecast period. The period \nbegins with a weak upper level low and modest troughing off the East \nCoast, and strong upper level ridging over the central CONUS, \nespecially the plains. With time, the East Coast troughing will \nfurther weaken and the ridging will shift eastward as troughing \nadvances into the western CONUS, with the ridge reaching us during \nthe weekend. Until the ridge arrives, we will have a dry \nnorthwesterly flow aloft. At the surface, high pressure or ridging \nbased mainly to our north will dominate our area. The flow aloft \ncould support a weak and dying cold front to move south toward the \nOhio Valley late in the week, but it will probably not get here, and \neven if it does it would not have any real effect. Even so, no \nreturn to meaningful warm air advection is foreseen either. One \nmodel difference to note-- the NAM is much colder with surface \ntemperatures (mainly highs) than the GFS or ECMWF on Wednesday and \nThursday. The NAM generates very low and shallow stratus in cold air \nadvection and would suggest high temperatures as much as 15-20 \ndegrees colder than the GFS or ECMWF. Considering the lack of precip \nor significant systems passing through, this scenario looks highly \nsuspect and is being discounted.\n\nLarger scale model differences finally become more significant early \nnext week in terms of the demise of the upper ridge and how \ntroughing evolves as it advances toward us from the west or \nnorthwest. However, in terms of actual effects on our local weather, \nthe differences don't appear to become significant until after this \nlong term period.",
